{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi James, sorry things are slow today. I checked your desktop and it has plenty of free disk space and no warnings, so storage isn't the problem. It also hasn't checked in with us recently, which could mean a network problem. Could you answer a few quick questions? 1) Is it only the shared drives that are slow, or are websites, Teams and email slow too? 2) Are your coworkers nearby having the same problem right now? 3) Is it one shared drive or folder, or all of them? 4) When did you last fully restart the computer? In the meantime, please check that the network cable is plugged in firmly at both ends, pause any big uploads or downloads (like OneDrive syncing), and do a full restart (Start > Power > Restart). Then let us know if it's still slow. If others are seeing it too, tell us and we'll pass it to our network team straight away.",
  "internal_note": "User 8, James O'Connor, Warehouse Supervisor (Operations), usual hours 06:00-14:00 CT. Account is active with no lockout and VPN access enabled. His only device is desktop BL-DT-0108 (Win 11). It shows OFFLINE. Last check-in was 2026-10-09T13:09:50Z. Disk is 400/512 GB free (78%) with no warnings, so KB-017 disk space is ruled out. Following KB-015/KB-019 quick checks, I've asked: shares only vs. everything, whether coworkers are affected, one share vs. all, and last restart. I also gave basic fix steps (check cable, pause sync, restart). The device being offline may point to a local network or connectivity issue. If several users in the office/warehouse report slowness, or one share is slow for everyone, escalate to Tier 2 per KB-015.",
